    name: 'Avak Crater',
    description: 'Avak is an impact crater centered approximately 12 km (7.5 mi) southeast of Utqiagvik, Alaska, United States. Avak is a subcircular structure about 12 km (7.5 mi) in diameter and 1 km (0.62 mi) deep. In the structure, metamorphic basement rocks and regionally flat lying sedimentary rocks are uplifted and intensely deformed. The Avak structure has no surface expression in the swampy, lake-dotted tundra of the Arctic coastal plain. The structure is covered by a thin veneer of permanently frozen Pliocene and Pleistocene rock. This means that the age of the impact took place anywhere between 3 million...\n\nAlternative Lore: According to alternative history theorists, Avak crater represents a ground-zero site of an ancient high-yield thermonuclear blast or particle-beam weapon strike predating recorded history. Proponents point to the extreme presence of shocked quartz and glass-like tektites as evidence of localized artificial vitrification rather than a natural kinetic impact.',
