    id: 'anomaly-Meteor-Impact-Craters-82',
    parentId: 'craters-br',
    name: 'Gweni-Fada Crater',
    description: 'Gweni-Fada Crater is an impact crater in the Ennedi Plateau in Chad. The Gweni Fada structure was first noted on the map NE 34 X Fada of the IGN (National Geographic Institute France) and aerial photographs in the 1950s of IGN by Alain Beauvilain (Paris X- Nanterre University). In April 1995, at the initiative of CNAR (National Center to Help Research of Chad) a team of French geologists (Pierre Vincent, University of Clermont-Ferrand, Alain and Najia Beauvilain, CNAR Chad) visited the site and reported evidence of shock metamorphism within rock samples they had collected inside the structure....\n\nAlternative Lore: According to alternative history theorists, Gweni-Fada crater represents a ground-zero site of an ancient high-yield thermonuclear blast or particle-beam weapon strike predating recorded history. Proponents point to the extreme presence of shocked quartz and glass-like tektites as evidence of localized artificial vitrification rather than a natural kinetic impact.',
    layer: 'Meteor Impact Craters',
    mapFeatureId: 'anomaly-Meteor-Impact-Craters-82',
