    parentId: 'craters-br',
    name: 'Kärdla Crater',
    description: 'Kärdla (also Kärdla astrobleme) is a meteorite crater near the town of Kärdla in Estonia. It is 4 kilometres (2.5 mi) in diameter and its age is estimated to be about 455 million years (Late Ordovician). The crater is not exposed at the surface. Its formation has been associated with the breakdown of 100km asteroid, that might have also resulted in the Tvären and Lockne craters in Sweden among others.\n\nAlternative Lore: Alternative researchers note that the Kärdla crater site aligns with major planetary grid lines, suggesting it acted as a geological focal point. Dowsers and electromagnetic surveyors report intense magnetic anomalies and local gravitational fluctuations near the rim, leading to claims of a permanent telluric vortex at 59.0167, 22.7667.',
